 "notes": "Figure 9(a) of the paper (e-print file decay_lims.png) gives the headline 3-sigma exclusion on the dark-matter radiative decay rate lambda [s^-1] vs frequency over 1330-2680 MHz (GBT L and S bands, Breakthrough Listen data). The dashed curve (two-photon final state, i.e. the axion case chi->gamma gamma) was digitized per 5-MHz limit window; the red-shaded frequency regions (1516-1658, 1888-1961, 2171-2258, 2306-2522 MHz), where no limit is set, are omitted, so the curve has gaps. Conversion to the axion-photon coupling uses Gamma(a->gamma gamma) = g_agamma^2 m_a^3/(64 pi) with m_a = 2 h nu (each photon carries m_a/2). The paper's limits assume an NFW halo (Nesti & Salucci) with local density 0.471 GeV/cm^3 and (V_S, sigma_vir) = (225, 250) km/s, and include the stimulated-emission enhancement 2 f_gamma. Confidence level is 3 sigma (p-value threshold), i.e. ~99.7%, not 90/95%. The paper itself quotes only lambda (and <sigma v> for annihilation); the g_agamma conversion is mine, exact for the two-photon decay the paper's dashed curve assumes.",
